# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether the static panel's unit runs; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. The node's
# post-transaction hook starts it once /etc/kgsm-web/panel.env names a host, and not before.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who disabled this unit keeps it disabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly undo that.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-web-static.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-web-static serves the Control Panel as a static site at one public name, with a Let's Encrypt
  certificate, and declares that origin to the auth anchor on this machine as a client of its sign-in.
  It serves nothing until the name is set:

    /etc/kgsm-web/panel.env      KGSM_PANEL_HOST=kgsm.example.com
    systemctl restart kgsm-web-static

  The name must point at this machine with ports 80 and 443 reaching it: the certificate is issued
  over nginx's webroot on port 80.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-web/panel.env, merge it — new settings are declared there
  and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  The panel's sites are no longer loaded; reload nginx to stop serving them, and restart
  kgsm-auth-anchor to drop the panel's origin from its clients. The certificate is left in
  /etc/letsencrypt and the rendered files in /var/lib/kgsm-web-static.

MSG
}
